.\" fsb(1): manual page. Hand-written roff (man(7) macros).
.\" Check with:  mandoc -Tlint man/fsb.1   and   man ./man/fsb.1
.TH FSB 1 "October 8, 2026" "fsb 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fsb \- filesystem browser: a read-only, local-only web view of your files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fsb
.RB [ \-\-debug ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-open ]
.RB [ \-\-background ]
.RB [ \-\-browser
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-allow\-system\-root ]
.RB [ \-\-root
.IR PATH ]...
.RI [ path ]
.br
.B fsb \-\-init
.br
.B fsb
.RB [ \-\-stop ]
.RB [ \-\-status ]
.br
.B fsb
.RB [ \-\-show\-deny ]
.RB [ \-\-show\-ignore ]
.br
.B fsb \-\-version
.br
.B fsb \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fsb
serves a web interface for browsing your own files on macOS.
It listens on the loopback address
.B 127.0.0.1
only, prints a single-use address, opens it in your browser, and runs until you
interrupt it.
In the browser you can list folders, sort, filter and search by name, and
preview
files (text and source code, JSON, CSV, Markdown, images, PDFs, the
contents of
zip and tar archives, and on macOS the Quick Look picture of iWork and Office
documents) without leaving the window.
.PP
.B fsb
is read-only.
It never modifies, creates, renames, moves or deletes anything it serves.
The only files it writes are its own, in
.IR ~/.config/fsb/ :
the rule files that
.B \-\-init
creates (never overwriting one), and the bookkeeping files listed under
.BR FILES .
.PP
Two rule files control what you see.
Paths matching the
.I ignore
rules are hidden from listings and search but remain reachable by their path.
Paths matching the
.I deny
rules are never served in any way, and are answered exactly as a path that
does not
exist.
A built-in set of deny rules covers known credential and secret locations, and
is
active even if you have no rule files.
.PP
Only the folders you name are served.
With no path,
.B fsb
serves your home folder.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be written with one or two leading hyphens.
.TP
.I path
The first root to serve.
It must be an existing folder.
At most one is accepted.
The default is your home folder.
.TP
.BI \-\-root " PATH"
Serve an additional root, such as
.BR /Volumes/Data .
May be repeated.
When there are several roots the interface offers a way to switch between them.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " N"
Use port
.I N
for this run only (not remembered for next time); 0 lets the OS choose.
Without this flag,
.B fsb
reuses the port it last used successfully (see
.B ~/.config/fsb/lastport
below), so the browser's stored preferences, which are kept per address
including the port, survive a restart; the first time there is nothing to
reuse yet, a free port is chosen and then remembered.
If the remembered port is now held by another program,
.B fsb
refuses to start rather than silently choosing a different one, and says to
retry with
.BR \-\-port .
Must be 0\-65535.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-system\-root
Permit
.B /
as a root.
Without this option
.B fsb
refuses to serve
.BR / ,
however it is reached: a root that is a symbolic link to
.BR / ,
or a home folder that resolves to it, is refused too.
The rules still apply.
On Linux,
.BR /proc ,
.B /sys
and
.B /dev
can be listed, but device files are never opened, and a process's environment
and command line in
.B /proc
show as empty.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-open
Print the launch URL and do not open a browser.
Use this to paste the URL into a browser of your choice.
.TP
.B \-\-background
Run in the background: print the startup lines, open the browser (unless
.BR \-\-no\-open ),
and return to the shell once
.B fsb
is serving.
It keeps running after the terminal is closed.
A startup error is reported as usual and nothing is left running.
Only one background
.B fsb
runs at a time.
Stop it with
.BR \-\-stop .
.TP
.B \-\-stop
Stop the running
.BR fsb ,
wait for it to exit, and exit.
That is the one started with
.BR \-\-background ,
or else an
.BR fsb ,
however it was started (for example in another terminal), that holds the port
remembered in
.IR ~/.config/fsb/lastport .
Only a process that is
.B fsb
is ever stopped.
Fails if none is running.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Say whether
.B fsb
is running, found as for
.BR \-\-stop ,
and if so whether in the background or the foreground, its address, process
number, start time and terminal; then exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-browser " NAME"
Open the launch URL in
.I NAME
instead of your default browser: on macOS an application (for example
.BR """Google Chrome""" ),
on Linux a program, run with the URL as its one argument (for example
.BR firefox ;
it takes no arguments of its own, so for options use a small script).
A name beginning with a hyphen, or containing a NUL, carriage return or
newline, is
refused.
If the browser cannot be opened,
.B fsb
says so and keeps running; the printed URL still works.
.IP
On Linux the default browser is opened with
.BR xdg-open (1),
and only when there is a display
.RB ( DISPLAY
or
.BR WAYLAND_DISPLAY ):
without one, as over
.BR ssh ,
.B xdg-open
could start a text browser that would use up the single-use URL itself, so
.B fsb
only prints it.
.B fsb
starts the browser in a session of its own, so Control-C in the terminal
stops
.B fsb
but not the browser, and with no terminal input or output.
An opener still running after 3 seconds, as a browser it started can be, is
taken to have succeeded.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Log verbosely to standard error, and answer a request for a denied path with a
body
naming the rule that matched (the status is still 404).
Nothing else reveals which rule matched.
.TP
.B \-\-init
Write the default rule files to
.I ~/.config/fsb/
and exit.
The folder is created with mode 0700 and each file with mode 0600.
A file that already exists is left unchanged and reported.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-deny
Print the deny rules in effect, one per line, and exit without serving.
A first comment line names their source: the deny file, or the built-in core
rules when there is no file.
A final comment line names any core rule the file leaves out.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-ignore
Print the ignore rules in effect in the same way, and exit.
Given together with
.BR \-\-show\-deny ,
both lists are printed, deny first.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and the commit
.B fsb
was built from, and exit.
The version comes from the git tag
.RB ( v1.0.0 ,
or a pseudo-version for a later commit); the commit is followed by
.B modified
if the source had uncommitted changes.
A program installed with
.B go install
from a tagged version has no commit recorded and names the tag instead.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit with status 0.
.SH STARTING AND STOPPING
On success
.B fsb
prints, on standard output, a line naming the roots and the address, then the
launch
URL:
.PP
.RS
.nf
fsb: serving /Users/you read-only on http://127.0.0.1:53349
fsb: open this single-use URL: http://127.0.0.1:53349/?token=...
fsb: runs until stopped (Control-C), or start it with --background to get your prompt back
.fi
.RE
.PP
The port is the one remembered from your last successful start, or a free one
the first time; see
.B ~/.config/fsb/lastport
under
.B FILES
and
.B \-\-port
above.
The URL works once: the first request that uses it sets a session cookie in your
browser and the token is then useless, so a copy left in your shell history or
browser
history cannot be used.
It then takes the browser to a random path prefix under which everything is
served (the launch URL itself does not name it); a bookmark does not survive a
restart.
.PP
.B fsb
runs in the foreground, like any other program that keeps serving requests
(a development web server, for example): it does not exit on its own, because
exiting would stop the browser from being able to reach it.
Interrupt it with Control-C or send
.BR SIGTERM ;
it stops accepting requests, allows requests in progress up to three seconds to
finish, and exits with status 0.
.PP
To get your shell prompt back without stopping
.BR fsb ,
start it with
.BR \-\-background ,
and stop it later with
.BR "fsb \-\-stop" .
With
.BR \-\-background ,
the last startup line names the process instead:
.PP
.RS
.nf
fsb: running in the background (process 4242); stop it with "fsb --stop"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Alternatively, use the shell's job control:
.RS
.nf
.RB "$ " "fsb &"
.RB "$ " "\&..."  "  (your prompt is free; fsb keeps running and keeps serving the browser)"
.RB "$ " fg        "  (brings fsb back to the foreground)"
.RB "^C"           "  (or, from the background, ""kill %1"" to stop it directly)"
.fi
.RE
A browser tab open on the printed URL keeps working the whole time
.B fsb
is running, in the foreground or in the background; only stopping the process
(by either method) ends the session.
.SH USING THE INTERFACE
.SS Keys
.TP
.BR Up ", " Down ", " PgUp ", " PgDn ", " Home ", " End
Move the selection.
.TP
.BR Enter " or " Right
Open the selected folder, which selects its first entry.
A search result opens in its folder with the file selected.
.TP
.BR Left " or " Backspace
Go up one folder, reselecting the one you left.
.TP
.B Space
Show or hide the preview pane.
.TP
.B /
Filter this folder by name.
.TP
.B Alt+S
Search subfolders by name.
Enter runs the search.
.TP
.B Alt+G
Go to a path.
Accepts an absolute path or one beginning with
.BR ~/ .
.TP
.B Alt+C
Copy the selected entry's path (the folder's, if nothing is selected).
.TP
.RI "any letter or digit"
Jump to the next entry whose name starts with it, as in a graphical file
manager: typing several keys quickly narrows the match, and typing the same
key again cycles to the next entry that still matches.
Search, Go to a path and Copy live on
.BR Alt ,
rather than the bare letter, precisely so every letter is free for this.
.TP
.B Escape
Clear the search, close a menu, or leave a text box.
.TP
.BR Alt+Left ", " Alt+Right
With a column header focused, move the column.
.PP
Shortcuts do not act while you are typing in a text box.
.SS Options in the window
.B Hidden files
shows names that begin with a dot (it never reveals an ignored or denied entry).
.B Folders first
lists folders before files under every sort column.
.B Match case
makes the filter and search case-sensitive.
.B Hover previews
shows the first lines of a file when you hold the pointer over it.
.B Preview pane
shows or hides the pane.
Column widths, order and visibility, and these choices, are remembered by the
browser itself, not sent to or stored by
.BR fsb .
The one thing
.B fsb
does keep on disk is the port it last used (see
.B \-\-port
and
.B ~/.config/fsb/lastport
under
.BR FILES ),
so that the browser is looking at the same address, and so the same stored
preferences, each time.
.SS Names
Two names are the same name when the file system says so: on the default
case-insensitive macOS volume, when they are equal after Unicode normalization
and full
case folding (so that the sharp s and
.BR ss ,
or a ligature and its letters, are the same).
The filter, search and every rule follow this.
A name that contains characters that would disguise it, such as a right-to-left
override or a newline, is shown with a visible marker such as
.BR <U+202E> .
A name that is not valid UTF-8, which Linux allows, shows each byte that does
not fit as a marker such as
.B <0xE9>
and can still be opened; Copy path then gives the path in shell quoting, such as
.BR $\(aqcaf\exE9.txt\(aq .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/fsb/ignore
Hide rules.
If absent, the built-in rules apply:
.BR .DS_Store ,
.B .git/
and
.BR node_modules/ .
.TP
.I ~/.config/fsb/deny
Block rules.
If absent, the core deny rules below apply.
If present it is authoritative: a core rule that it lacks is not enforced, and
.B fsb
warns about it.
.PP
Both files are read once, at startup, and are global; there are no per-folder
rule
files.
A file that cannot be parsed stops
.B fsb
from starting.
.TP
.I ~/.config/fsb/lastport
The port used at the last successful start.
Written automatically; not created by
.BR \-\-init .
A number on its own line, safe to edit or delete by hand: deleting it, or
changing the number, just changes which port is tried next time.
Ignored, and never written to, when
.B \-\-port
is given.
Unlike the rule files, a
.I lastport
that cannot be parsed does not stop
.BR fsb :
it is treated as if the file were absent, and a free port is chosen as usual.
.TP
.I ~/.config/fsb/pid
The process number of the
.B fsb
started with
.BR \-\-background ,
for
.BR \-\-stop .
Removed when it stops.
.TP
.I ~/.config/fsb/background.log
What the background
.B fsb
writes after startup, such as
.B \-\-debug
output.
Replaced at each
.B \-\-background
start.
.SH RULE SYNTAX
The syntax is that of
.BR gitignore (5).
A line is blank, a comment (first character
.BR # ),
or a rule.
Surrounding white space is ignored, and a leading byte order mark and any line
ending
are accepted.
.TP
.B *
Any characters except
.BR / .
.TP
.B ?
One character except
.BR / .
.TP
.BR [abc] ", " [!abc]
A character class (which never matches
.BR / ).
.TP
.B **
Any number of folders:
.B **/name
matches at any depth.
.TP
.B trailing /
The rule applies to folders only.
.TP
.B \e#
A literal
.BR # .
.TP
.B !
Negation, allowed in the ignore file only.
.PP
A pattern with no slash matches a name at any depth.
A pattern beginning
.B ~/
is relative to your home folder; one beginning with
.B /
is absolute; one containing a slash elsewhere is relative to your home folder.
Matching ignores letter case and normalization as described under
.BR Names ,
and ignores trailing white space in a name.
.PP
An excluded folder excludes everything in it.
Deny wins over ignore, and nothing in the ignore file can expose a denied path.
.PP
A rule file must work as written or fail loudly.
.B fsb
refuses to start, naming the file, line and problem, for negation in the deny
file, an
empty pattern, a trailing backslash, a
.B /
inside
.BR [...] ,
.BI ~ name
(another user's home), a pattern in quotes, an inline comment (write
.B \e#
for a literal
.BR # ),
an invisible or formatting character, and a character that looks like a slash
but is
not one.
.SH DEFAULT DENY RULES
These core rules are built in and match at any depth, so the same folders in a
backup,
in a clone of your home folder or in another account are protected too:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&.ssh/  .aws/  .gnupg/  .kube/
**/.config/gh/
**/Library/Keychains/
**/Library/Application Support/Google/Chrome*/
**/Library/Application Support/Chromium/
**/Library/Application Support/Firefox/
**/Library/Application Support/BraveSoftware/
**/Library/Application Support/Microsoft Edge*/
**/Library/Application Support/Vivaldi/
**/Library/Application Support/com.operasoftware.Opera*/
**/Library/Thunderbird/
**/Library/Safari/  **/Library/Cookies/
**/.config/google\-chrome*/  **/.config/chromium/  **/.mozilla/
**/.config/BraveSoftware/  **/.config/microsoft\-edge*/
**/.config/mozilla/  **/.config/vivaldi*/  **/.config/opera*/
**/.thunderbird/  **/.pki/nssdb/  **/snap/chromium/common/chromium/
**/.var/app/*/config/google\-chrome*/  **/.var/app/*/config/chromium/
**/.var/app/*/config/BraveSoftware/  **/.var/app/*/config/microsoft\-edge*/
**/.var/app/*/config/mozilla/  **/.var/app/*/config/vivaldi*/
**/.var/app/*/config/opera*/  **/.var/app/*/data/keyrings/
**/.local/share/keyrings/  **/.local/share/kwalletd/
**/.password\-store/
\&.netrc  .git\-credentials  .pgpass
\&.env  .env.*  *.pem  *.key
id_rsa  id_dsa  id_ecdsa  id_ed25519  id_ecdsa_sk  id_ed25519_sk
*.p12  *.pfx  *.ppk  *.jks  *.keystore
*.kdbx  *.keychain  *.keychain\-db
.fi
.RE
.PP
Public key files such as
.B id_rsa.pub
are not denied.
The secret-file patterns are deliberately broad:
.B *.key
also matches Keynote presentations,
.B .env
also matches a folder of that name (such as a Python virtual environment), and
.B .env.*
also matches templates such as
.BR .env.example .
If you need one of them, remove the line from your deny file; you will get the
warning
below.
.PP
Optional rules, commented out in the file
.B \-\-init
writes, are
.BR ~/.docker/config.json ,
.BR ~/.npmrc ,
.B ~/Library/Mail/
and
.BR ~/Library/Messages/ .
.PP
If your deny file lacks a core rule, at startup
.B fsb
prints
.PP
.RS
.nf
fsb: warning: core deny rule(s) disabled: .ssh/, .aws/; these paths are browsable.
.fi
.RE
.PP
and the interface shows a banner with the same message until every core rule
is back in
effect.
This cannot be turned off.
.PP
A file with several names (a hard link) is judged by its identity: if any name
is
denied, every name is refused and unlisted.
The names are found by an index built in the background at startup (about 20
seconds
for a home folder of 700,000 files); until it is ready, files that have
several names
are refused.
After that, a file with several names is served only if the index has found
every one of its names, none is denied, and each is still that file, with no
symbolic link along its path, when it is used.
A file with a name outside the roots and the credential folders, or in a
folder
.B fsb
cannot read, is therefore refused, as is one whose names changed (a link
added or removed, or a file or folder renamed or moved) until the index is
refreshed; refreshes start at least 15 seconds apart, and at least twice as
long as the last one took.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Your home folder.
It is resolved to its real location and determines the default root, the
location of
the configuration folder, and the meaning of
.B ~
in rules.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal shutdown, or
.BR \-\-help ,
or
.BR \-\-init .
.TP
.B 1
Any error, reported on standard error as one line beginning
.BR fsb: .
No server is started.
.SH SECURITY
.B fsb
is designed so that a web page cannot use it.
It listens only on 127.0.0.1; it refuses requests whose
.B Host
is not exactly the loopback address and port, and any request that carries
another
origin (including a page on another port of the same host).
The session cookie is
.B HttpOnly
and
.BR SameSite=Strict ,
and is scoped to a random path prefix, so that the browser never sends it to
another web
server on 127.0.0.1 that does not know the prefix.
The launch URL does not contain the prefix; only the redirect that answers it
does, so whoever first uses the launch URL learns it.
On Linux that can only be your own browser (see below); on macOS another user
would have to read the URL and use it in the moment
.B open
runs.
On Linux, a request with the session cookie is also answered only on a
connection of
.BR fsb 's
own user, so a cookie captured by another user is useless; see LIMITATIONS.
Only
.B GET
and
.B HEAD
are answered.
Files are never rendered from the interface's own origin except as the previews
described above; downloads are always sent as attachments.
.B fsb
makes no outbound connection, sends no telemetry, and never causes a
cloud-only file to
be downloaded.
A denied path is indistinguishable from a missing one.
.PP
For a Numbers, Pages, Word, Excel or PowerPoint document, the preview is the
picture macOS draws of it, made by running
.BR qlmanage (1)
on a private copy that
.B fsb
reads after the same checks as any other preview; it is the one place
.B fsb
runs another program on your files.
Quick Look never sees your own file, so a Finder alias named like a document
cannot lead it anywhere.
A document stored only in the cloud is not copied, nor is a package document
with a part stored only in the cloud; parts of a package that a listing would
leave out (denied files, links) are left out of the copy.
Keynote documents
.RI ( .key )
match the core deny rule
.B *.key
and are not shown at all.
.PP
Anything that runs as you can read the same files:
.B fsb
is not a sandbox against local malware.
See the security policy shipped with the source for the threat model, the
guarantees and
the known limitations.
.SH EXAMPLES
Browse your home folder:
.PP
.RS
.B fsb
.RE
.PP
Browse one folder, and open it in Chrome:
.PP
.RS
.B fsb \-\-browser """Google Chrome""" ~/Documents
.RE
.PP
Serve two folders and paste the URL yourself:
.PP
.RS
.B fsb \-\-no\-open \-\-root /Volumes/Data ~/src
.RE
.PP
Preferences already survive a restart on their own; use a specific port just
this once, perhaps to work around another program that is using the usual one:
.PP
.RS
.B fsb \-\-port 4488
.RE
.PP
Write the rule files, then edit them:
.PP
.RS
.nf
fsb \-\-init
$EDITOR ~/.config/fsb/deny
.fi
.RE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B fsb: refusing to serve / without \-\-allow\-system\-root
Serving the whole system needs the explicit option.
.TP
.B fsb: deny rules: FILE: line N (...): MESSAGE
A rule file could not be parsed, and
.B fsb
did not start.
Fix the line named.
.TP
.B fsb: port N (used last time) is in use by ...
Something else is now using the port
.B fsb
remembered from before.
The message names the program holding it, with its process number, when it
can be found.
If it is another
.B fsb
(often one left running in another terminal), stop it with
.BR "fsb \-\-stop" .
Otherwise run again with
.B \-\-port
and a different number.
The remembered port itself is left unchanged, so plain
.B fsb
tries it again next time, and works again once the other program is done
with it.
.TP
.B fsb: could not open the browser
The application or program named by
.B \-\-browser
was not found, or on Linux
.B xdg-open
is missing (it comes with
.BR xdg-utils )
or failed (status 3: a program it needs is missing; status 4: opening
failed).
The server is running; open the printed URL yourself.
.TP
.B fsb: warning: refused the launch URL: it was opened by another user
On Linux, a connection of another user tried the launch URL, which it may have
read from a command line.
It was refused and the URL still works for you; another user on this machine
is trying to reach your files.
After three such warnings, further refused launches are not reported; refused
requests with the session are counted separately.
.TP
.B fsb: warning: refused the launch URL: fsb runs as root
.B fsb
was started as root (for example with
.BR sudo ),
so your browser's connection is not its user's.
Start
.B fsb
as yourself.
.TP
.B fsb: warning: refused a request: a request with your session came from another user
On Linux, another user presented your session cookie, which a server of theirs
may have captured from your browser.
The request was refused, and your own browser keeps working; restart
.B fsb
to start a new session.
.TP
.B fsb: warning: refused a request: could not tell which user sent a request with your session
On Linux,
.B fsb
could not find the owner of a connection that carried your session cookie
(see the launch case below); the next request tries again.
.TP
.B fsb: warning: refused the launch URL: could not tell which user opened it
On Linux,
.B fsb
could not find the owner of the connection (no
.BR /proc ,
WSL, a user namespace that hides other users), so it refuses the launch URL
on this system.
.TP
.B Not found. This path is outside the folders fsb serves
Shown in the interface for a path outside every root; start
.B fsb
with a different path, or add
.BR \-\-root .
.SH LIMITATIONS
A file with several names is refused when one of its names lies outside every
root and outside the credential folders, even if no name is denied: for
example a package that
.B pnpm
links from its store in
.B ~/Library/pnpm
when that folder is not under a root, or an object file of a
.B git clone \-\-local
whose source lies outside the roots.
When
.B fsb
opens the browser, the single-use URL is in the command line of the program it
runs
.RB ( xdg-open ,
the
.B \-\-browser
program and the browser it starts on Linux;
.B open
on macOS, briefly), where another user of the same machine can read it.
On Linux that user still cannot use it directly:
.B fsb
accepts the launch URL only from a connection of its own user, warns
.RB ( "refused the launch URL" ),
and the URL keeps working for your browser.
A connection through a port forward or proxy counts as the user running it,
so a forward you run lets through whoever can reach it.
Where the owner of a connection cannot be determined (no
.BR /proc ,
WSL, some sandboxes), the launch URL is refused and
.B fsb
cannot be used.
On macOS, which offers no such check, it is visible only while
.B open
runs; on a shared Mac use
.B \-\-no\-open
